<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aten</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aten was the disc of the sun worshipped as a god in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, depicted as a sun whose rays end in human hands. Already honoured under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a>, it was raised by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> in the fourteenth century BCE above all other gods, whose cults, especially that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, were suppressed. After Akhenaten's death the traditional cults were restored and the Aten's temples were dismantled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aten.jpg" alt="Aten" width="320" height="139" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AtonX · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aten.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
